In this video, you see how you can take these representations and plug them into NLP applications.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.000
Let's start with an example.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.880
Continuing with the named entity recognition example, if you're trying to detect people's

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.680
names, given a sentence like Sally Johnson is an orange farmer, hopefully you figure

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.480
out that Sally Johnson is a person's name, hence the output's 1 like that.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.360
And one way to be sure that Sally Johnson has to be a person, rather than say the name of

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.600
a corporation, is that your orange farmer is a person.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.920
So previously, we had talked about one-hot representations to represent these words, x1,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.000
x2, and so on.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.120
But if you can now use the featureized representations, the embedding vectors that we talked about

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.840
in the last video, then after having trained a model that uses word embeddings as the inputs,

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:04.960
if you now see a new input, Robert Lin is an apple farmer, knowing that orange and apple

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.640
are very similar will make it easier for your learning algorithm to generalize to figure

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:15.520
out that Robert Lin is also a human, is also a person's name.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.400
One of the most interesting cases will be what if in your test set, you see not Robert

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.600
Lin as apple farmer, but you see much less common words.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:31.980
What if you see Robert Lin is a durian cultivator.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:38.900
A durian is a rare type of fruit, popular in Singapore and a few other countries.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.580
But if you have a small label training set for the name entity recognition task, you

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:48.340
might not even have seen the word durian or seen the word cultivator in your training

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.340
set.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.660
I guess technically, this should be a durian cultivator.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.100
But if you have learned a word embedding that tells you that durian is a fruit, so it's

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.380
kind of like an orange, and a cultivator, you know, some of the cultivators is kind

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.100
of like a farmer, then you might still be able to generalize from having seen an

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.540
orange farmer in your training set to knowing that a durian cultivator is also probably

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.740
a person.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.580
So one of the reasons that word embeddings will be able to do this is the algorithm

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.020
so learning word embeddings can examine very large text corpuses, maybe found off

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.020
the internet.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.620
So you can examine very large data sets, maybe a billion words, maybe even up to a

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.740
hundred billion words.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.060
This would be quite reasonable.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.540
So very large training sets of just unlabeled text.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.780
And by examining tons of unlabeled text, which you can download, you know, more or

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:49.860
less for free, you can figure out that orange and durian are kind of similar and farmer

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.660
and cultivator are kind of similar, and therefore learn embeddings that groups them

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.660
together.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:03.340
Now, having discovered that orange and durian are both fruits, by reading massive amounts

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.620
of internet text, what you can do is then take this word embedding and apply it to

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:12.620
your name entity recognition task for which you might have a much smaller training set,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.820
maybe just a hundred thousand words in your training set or even much smaller.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:24.060
And so this allows you to carry out transfer learning, where you take information you've

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.540
learned from huge amounts of unlabeled text that you can set down essentially for free

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:36.420
off the internet to figure out that orange apple and durian are fruits and then transfer

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.260
that knowledge to a task such as name entity recognition for which you might have a relatively

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.620
small labeled training set.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:52.140
Oh, and of course, for simplicity, I drew this as a for and only as a uni directional RNN.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.660
If you actually want to carry out the name entity recognition task, you should of course

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.140
use a bi directional RNN rather than a simpler one after one here.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:06.060
But to summarize, this is how you can carry out transfer learning using word embeddings.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:12.180
Step one is to learn word embeddings from a large tech corpus, very large tech corpora,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.620
or you can also download pre trained word embeddings online.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:23.340
There are several word embeddings that you can find online under a very permissive licenses.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.260
And you can then take these word embeddings and transfer the embedding to a new task

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.860
we have a much smaller labeled training set and use this, let's say 300 dimensional

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.620
embedding to represent your words.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:42.220
One nice thing also about this is you can now use relatively lower dimensional feature

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:43.420
vectors.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:50.580
So rather than using a 10,000 dimensional one hot vector, you can now instead use maybe

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:57.300
a 300 dimensional dense vector, although the one hot vector is sparse and the 300 dimensional

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.580
vector that you might learn through embedding will be a dense vector.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:07.700
And then finally, as you train your model on your new tasks on your name entity recognition

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:13.220
tasks, with a smaller label data set, one thing you can optionally do is to continue

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:20.060
to find to continue to adjust the word embeddings with the new data.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:25.340
In practice, you would do this only if your new task only if this task two has a pretty

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.100
big data set.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:33.300
If your label data set for step two is quite small, then usually I would not bother to continue

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.780
to find you the word embeddings.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.700
So when embeddings tend to make the biggest difference when the task you're trying to

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.940
carry out has a relatively small training set.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.860
So it has been useful for many NLP tasks.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:51.820
Now just name a few don't worry if you don't know these terms, it has been useful for

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.980
name entity recognition for text summarization for core reference resolution for pausing.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.780
These are all maybe pretty standard NLP tasks.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.340
It has been less useful for language modeling machine translation, especially if you're

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.060
actually a language modeling machine translation task for which you have a lot of data just

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:11.980
dedicated to that task.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:16.900
So as you see in other transfer learning settings, if you're trying to transfer from some task

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:23.820
A to some task B, the process of transfer learning is just most useful when you happen

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:28.980
to have a ton of data for A and a relatively smaller data set for B.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:35.540
And so that's true for a lot of NLP tasks and just less true for some language modeling

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:37.780
and machine translation settings.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:44.220
Finally, word embeddings has an interesting relationship to the face encoding set of

