videos, whereas what we'll do for learning word embeddings is that we'll have a fixed

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:04.340
vocabulary of say 10,000 words and we'll learn a vector e1 through say e10,000 that

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:12.460
just learns a fixed encoding or learns a fixed embedding for each of the words in our vocabulary.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:18.100
So that's one difference between the set of ideas you solve for face recognition versus

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.340
what the algorithms will discuss in the next few videos.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:26.300
But the terms encoding and embedding are used somewhat interchangeably.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:31.460
So the difference I just described is not represented by the difference in terminology.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:36.900
It's just a difference in how we need to use these algorithms in face recognition, where

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:42.300
there's an unlimited sea of pictures you can see in the future versus natural language

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:47.460
processing where there might be just a fixed vocabulary and everything outside that will

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.340
just declare as an unknown word.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:55.940
So in this video, you saw how using word embeddings allows you to implement this type of transfer

